Upstream PR pingdotgg/t3code#34005 (branch feat/queue-send-all, 8a3911e583,
600af973b8, 7e554681ce, 1cc1223dea), squashed.

Behaviours:
- With two or more queued messages the queue footer shows two actions next to
  the "Show all N queued" toggle (the toggle still appears only when the list
  is collapsible): "Send one by one" (ghost) and "Send all as one" (outline,
  arrow-up icon). Each sits in a tooltip that says what it will do or why it
  is disabled. A single queued message shows neither.
- Send all as one dispatches a new server command, queued-run.merge, with the
  queued run ids in delivery order (at least two). The server folds the queue
  into its first message: texts joined by blank lines in order, attachments
  and context records moved onto the first message, and the other queued runs
  cancelled. On success the client sends the first run through the existing
  send-now path: resume when the thread is stopped, promote-to-steer into the
  running turn otherwise. A failed merge sends nothing.
- Merging keeps an "Ultrathink:" prefix once at the top when any part has it,
  drops attachment-only bootstrap prompts from the body and restores the
  bootstrap prompt when the merged message has attachments but no text.
- The server rejects a merge, leaving the queue as it was, with the reason as
  the command's rejection cause when: a run id is not queued ("Run <id> is not
  queued."), the ids are not a contiguous slice of the delivery queue in order
  (a message queued, moved or removed since the client looked, or a queued
  notification between them: "The queue changed. Review it and try again."),
  a part is a guest's message, a message from an agent or automation
  (non-user creator, scheduled task, sender thread, idle compaction, workflow
  script, delegated completion, notification), a slash command, a
  fresh-session message, a plan or continuation message, or uses another model
  than the first; parts share an attachment; the attachments exceed the
  provider limit; two parts carry different context records with the same id;
  the combined context exceeds the record limit, or exceeds the serialized
  context limit the event store enforces ("Queued messages carry too much
  context to send as one.", instead of a generic failure at commit).
  Delegated-completion runs are not part of the queue the command sees.
- Send all as one is disabled with a reason while a queued message is edited
  or saving, while another queue action runs, when the environment does not
  advertise queuedRunMerge in its server config ("Update this environment to
  send queued messages as one."), when a notification waits between the user's
  queued messages ("A notification is waiting between these messages."), when
  the messages cannot be combined (the server's reasons above), or when the
  running turn cannot take a message ("The current turn can't take messages
  right now."). On agents that steer by restarting the turn it stays enabled,
  so the whole queue costs one restart. During a compaction it skips the
  compaction.
- Send one by one resumes the whole queue when the thread is stopped (one
  resume command), or promotes each queued message into the running turn in
  order and stops at the first failure. It is disabled with a reason when the
  turn cannot take messages, during a compaction ("Send them as one to skip
  the compaction."), on agents that restart their turn for every steer ("This
  agent restarts its turn for every message. Send them as one instead."), and
  when a queued message cannot join the running turn as it is (fresh session,
  slash command, another model than the running turn, or attachments the
  provider does not accept while steering).
- Queue actions run one at a time: a single in-flight guard and busy run id
  cover Send now, Send all as one and Send one by one.
- A new keybinding command thread.sendQueuedMessagesAsOne ("Queue: Send All
  Queued as One Message" in Keybindings settings, no default shortcut) sends
  all as one from the chat view; the tooltip shows its shortcut when bound. It
  does nothing on a draft route or when the action is disabled.
- The server advertises queuedRunMerge: true in its config, counts the command
  as the queue.merge feature in telemetry, and rejects a merge of a queued
  guest message like an edit of one.
- isSlashCommandText moves into @t3tools/shared/model and is reused by the
  Claude ultrathink prefix and by the queue checks.
- The composer and keybindings user guides describe both actions, when Send
  all as one is unavailable, and the new keybinding.

Port to e07585e5fb:
- threadWorkflows.ts: upstream #34581 lets a queued message steer a preparing or starting run, delivered after setup, and also when the agent has not reported capabilities yet. canPromoteToSteer takes that rule. Send all as one follows it: a run still setting up takes the merged message, also with unknown capabilities. Send one by one keeps needing known active steering, so with unknown capabilities it stays disabled with the restart reason.
- threadWorkflows.test.ts: the "turn not yet steerable" row now uses a waiting run, which still cannot take messages; new rows cover a starting run and a preparing run without capabilities.
- QueuedRunsControl.tsx: upstream added the same useEnvironment import for linked senders, so this commit no longer adds a second one. Upstream's setup-steering test fixture gains model selections, which the send-each check compares now that a preparing run can take messages.

Port to ee4ef06469:
- Orchestrator.ts: upstream #29699 deletes the attachments an edit of a queued message removed. dispatchQueuedRunEdit keeps that cleanup after the shared emitQueuedMessageRewrite; a merge moves attachments onto the first message and removes none.
- QueuedRunsControl.browser.test.tsx: upstream #24055 feeds the thread state through real detail atoms and adds a streaming re-render case. The queue-action cases follow that case and no longer set a projection.

Port to f7b0b3c4d7: QueuedRunsControl.browser.test.tsx: the imports keep upstream #33298's useState, ExpandedImageDialog and ExpandedImagePreview (queued images open in the image viewer) next to this commit's createRef and QueuedRunsControlHandle.

Port to 6b134a7add: runtimeLayer.test.ts conflicted only in its contracts import list: upstream b79d277cdd (#37793) imports OrchestrationV2DomainEvent beside the OrchestrationV2Command type this commit imports; both are kept and the change itself is unchanged.
useQueuedRunEdit.browser.test.tsx: upstream #37507's steerSavedRun derives the queue state, which this commit's send-each check reads model selections from, so the fixture's runs gain a model selection like every real run has.

Port to 25875c11b2: Upstream #31283 (8753e4d36b) shows a toast with the server's reason when Send now fails. QueuedRunsControl.tsx: that toast now sits in this commit's shared sendNow helper, so Send now keeps upstream's message and the send after a merge and each step of Send one by one report a refused send the same way; Send one by one still stops at the first failure. QueuedRunsControl.browser.test.tsx: the command mock returns queued results or success, failures are real command failures with a cause (built by commandFailure), and upstream's Send now case renders inside its ToastProvider next to this commit's queue-action cases.

Port to b6ac2449f1: the copy follows upstream's translation (055b44919e) with its English unchanged: the queue buttons, tooltips and blocked reasons use gt() and <T> (Show all keeps upstream's plural); deriveThreadQueueWorkflowState's reasons are msg() through upstream's resolver; packages/shared/src/queuedMessageMerge.ts names its reasons in QUEUED_MESSAGE_MERGE_REASONS and the new client-only packages/shared/src/queuedMessageMergeCopy.ts translates them, so the server keeps English as docs/internals/i18n.md requires; the keybinding label joins upstream's msg() table.

Port to 1ed4f6a96f: upstream #38874 lets a run ask for a typed result (outputSchema) and refuses to steer such a run. Orchestrator.ts: dispatchQueuedRunEdit keeps upstream's check that an edited result request is not a command, then the shared emitQueuedMessageRewrite; the dispatch switch keeps run.output.submit next to queued-run.merge. queuedMessageMerge.ts refuses a part that asks for a result ("A message that asks for a result is sent on its own.", translated in queuedMessageMergeCopy.ts), so a merge never cancels or rewrites a result request; threadWorkflows.ts treats such a message as one that cannot join the running turn for Send one by one. runtimeLayer.test.ts and threadWorkflows.test.ts gain a row for it.

Port to 047fb5b162: upstream c385377544 (#40316, slash commands sent during a turn wait instead of steering) clears a queued run's steer-when-ready mark when an edit turns its message into a command. Orchestrator.ts: dispatchQueuedRunEdit keeps that run.updated after this commit's shared emitQueuedMessageRewrite (which now emits the message and turn-item updates the edit used to emit inline) and imports upstream's isSlashCommand beside mergeQueuedMessages; a merge already refuses a command part, so the merged message never needs it. contracts server.ts: queuedRunMerge sits beside upstream 10586f9e31's featureFlags.

Port to def64e3a54: upstream c3a330d834 (#40767, Queue shows for running sessions that cannot steer) counts an agent as able to steer only when it steers actively or can interrupt and restart (supportsSteering, canSteer). threadWorkflows.ts computes canPromoteToSteer with that rule before the send-as-one and send-each reasons read it. threadWorkflows.test.ts keeps upstream's supportsInterrupt capability beside this commit's activeSteeringAcceptsAttachments, and the agents in the send-the-whole-queue table report interrupt support.

queuedMessageMerge.ts: since upstream 08804b2170 (#40373) a queued message that names no model carries followsThreadSelection and runs on the thread's pick when it starts, while its run keeps the pick from queue time. A merge now refuses a part whose followsThreadSelection differs from the first part's ("Queued messages use different models."), so messages merge only when all of them or none of them follow the thread's pick. threadWorkflows.test.ts gains rows for both.

Port to a6c69be8df: upstream d572ce9afe (#41814, preserve model choices when editing queued messages) lets queued-run.edit carry a modelSelection. Orchestrator.ts: dispatchQueuedRunEdit takes upstream's single run.updated (the edited model selection and provider instance, plus the cleared steer-when-ready mark for a command or a limit) before this commit's shared emitQueuedMessageRewrite, which rewrites the message without followsThreadSelection when the edit picked a model, as upstream does. A merge keeps the first run and its model selection, so an edited model choice survives Send all as one; parts whose model selections or followsThreadSelection differ (an edited message beside one that follows the thread's pick, or two edited to different models) are still refused with "Queued messages use different models.". runtimeLayer.test.ts gains a case: a merge across one edited and one unedited message is refused and leaves the queue as it was, and after both are edited to the same model the merge keeps that model.

